# How many hours did a German work in 1870, compared with today?
subtitle: "Two hosts, one bar to drag: guess how long Germans worked in 1870, then see what cut the hours by sixty percent, and who works least today."
tags: ["economics","history","data"]
format: quiz
thumb: "band \"Land of hard work?\" stamp \"PLOT TWIST\""

## How many hours did a German work in 1870, compared with today?
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - '1870'
      - '1913'
      - '1960'
      - '1990'
      - '2023'
    values:
      - 3284
      - 2723
      - 2181
      - 1583
      - 1335
    value_labels: true
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 5000
    y_label: Hours worked a year
    box: full
    label_size: 30
sources:
    - id: owid
      title: Annual working hours per worker
      authors: Our World in Data, from Huberman & Minns (2007) and the Penn World Table
      year: 2025
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/annual-working-hours-per-worker
      finding: 'Germany: 3,284 hours in 1870, 2,723 in 1913, 2,181 in 1960, 1,583 in 1990, 1,335 in 2023, the lowest of all countries in the data that year; 2023: South Korea 1,910, United States 1,789'
    - id: owid_wh
      title: Working Hours
      authors: Charlie Giattino, Esteban Ortiz-Ospina and Max Roser
      year: 2020
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/working-hours
      finding: working hours have fallen dramatically over the last 150 years; workers in richer, more productive countries tend to work fewer hours

    card title "How long did a German work in 1870?"

A: How many hours a year did a German worker put in back in 1870? Start with today: about thirteen hundred hours.
    draw axes bar_5

A: That sounds like a lot, but spread over the year, holidays and part-timers included, it's about twenty-six hours a week.
    text wk26 "≈ 26 h/week" above bar_5 gap 55 #2f6b8f cites ["owid"] font_size 40

    erase wk26

    ask "Drag the 1870 bar to your guess." on bar_1 store g default "2000" relative true tolerance 0.1
        right: Well judged. Over three thousand hours.
        wrong: It was over three thousand hours a year.

B: Wait. Over three thousand? That's more than double!
    highlight bar_1

A: About sixty-three hours a week: long days, six days a week, almost no holidays.
    text wk63 "≈ 63 h/week" #b5482e at.data [2.15,4000] cites ["owid"] font_size 40

A: And then it fell, decade after decade.
    draw bar_2 bar_3 bar_4

B: So what brought it down? Strikes?
    highlight bar_3

    erase wk63

A: Unions and laws helped. But mostly productivity: each hour made far more, so people could buy back their time.
    text prod "more output per hour" #2f6b8f at.data [2.9,4100] cites ["owid_wh"] font_size 44

    erase prod

B: Still, Germans are the hard workers of Europe, right?
    highlight bar_5

A: Funny you say that. In this data, Germany now has the fewest working hours of any country.
    scratch cmp top-right cites ["owid"] work ["Hours a year, 2023","South Korea  1,910","USA  1,789","Germany  1,335: lowest"] font_size 30

B: So the famously hard-working Germans work the least?
    highlight bar_5 effect circle

A: Fewest hours, lots made in each one. Rich countries tend to work less, not more.
    highlight bar_5

